<!-- 1 syllable words !-->

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|xno|en}} {{m|fro|reuser}}, {{m|fro|ruser}}, originally used in English of hawks shaking the feathers of the body, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|recusare}}, by loss of the medial 'c.' Related to Provencal {{m|la|reusar}}.

Figurative meaning "to stir up, provoke to activity" is from 1580s; that of "awaken" is first recorded 1590s.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|rouze}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}


# An [[arousal]].
# {{lb|en|military|British|and|Canada}} The [[sounding]] of a [[bugle]] in the morning after [[reveille]], to [[signal]] that soldiers are to [[rise]] from [[bed]], often ''the rouse''.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|rous}}

# To [[wake]] (someone) or be [[awoken]] from [[sleep]], or from [[apathy]].
#* {{circa|1605}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Macbeth}}'', Act III, Scene 2,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=macbeth&Act=3&Scene=2&Scope=scene]</sup>
#*: Good things of day begin to droop and drowse;
#*: Night’s black agents to their preys do '''rouse'''.
#* '''1687''', {{w|Francis Atterbury}}, ''An Answer to Some Considerations on the Spirit of Martin Luther'', Oxford, pp. 41-42,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A26149.0001.001]</sup>
#*: As for the heat, with which he treated his other adversaries, ’twas sometimes strain’d a little too far, but in the general was extremely well fitted by the Providence of God to '''rowse''' up a people, the most phlegmatic of any in Christendome.
#* '''1713''', {{w|Alexander Pope}}, ''Ode for Musick'', London: Bernard Lintott, stanza 2, p. 3,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004809282.0001.000]</sup>
#*: At Musick, ''Melancholy'' lifts her Head;
#*: Dull ''{{w|Morpheus}}'' '''rowzes''' from his Bed;
#* '''1979''', ''{{w|Bernard Malamud}}'', ''{{w|Dubin's Lives|Dubin’s Lives}}'', New York: Farrar Straus Giroux, Chapter Eight, p. 284,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/dubinslives00mala]</sup>
#*: Dubin slept through the ringing alarm, aware of Kitty trying to '''rouse''' him and then letting him sleep.
# To [[cause]], [[stir up]], [[excite]] (a feeling, thought, etc.).
#: ''to '''rouse''' the faculties, passions, or emotions''
#* '''1719''', {{w|Daniel Defoe}}, ''{{w|The Farther Adventures of Robinson Crusoe}}'', London: W. Taylor, p. 127,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004784951.0001.000]</sup>
#*: {{...}} their first Step in Dangers, after the common Efforts are over, was always to despair, lie down under it, and die, without '''rousing''' their Thoughts up to proper Remedies for Escape.
#* '''1848''', {{w|Anne Brontë}}, ''{{w|The Tenant of Wildfell Hall}}'', London: John Murray, 1900, Chapter 27,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/969/969-h/969-h.htm]</sup>
#*: ‘You may think it all very fine, Mr. Huntingdon, to amuse yourself with '''rousing''' my jealousy; but take care you don’t '''rouse''' my hate instead.  And when you have once extinguished my love, you will find it no easy matter to kindle it again.’
#* '''1961''', {{w|V. S. Naipaul}}, ''{{w|A House for Mr Biswas}}'', Penguin, 1992, Part Two, Chapter 5, p. 494,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/houseformrbiswas00vsna_1]</sup>
#*: {{...}} he had grown to look upon houses as things that concerned other people, like churches, butchers’ stalls, cricket matches and football matches. They had ceased to '''rouse''' ambition or misery. He had lost the vision of the house.
# {{senseid|en|provoke}} To [[provoke]] (someone) to [[action]] or [[anger]].
#* '''1667''', {{w|John Milton}}, ''{{w|Paradise Lost}}'', Book 2, lines 284-287,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A50919.0001.001]</sup>
#*: He scarce had finisht, when such murmur filld
#*: Th’ Assembly, as when hollow Rocks retain
#*: The sound of blustring winds, which all night long
#*: Had '''rous’d''' the Sea {{...}}
#* '''1818''', {{w|Jane Austen}}, ''{{w|Persuasion (novel)|Persuasion}}'', Chapter 12,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/105/105-h/105-h.htm]</sup>
#*: “A surgeon!” said Anne.
#*: He caught the word; it seemed to '''rouse''' him at once, and saying only—“True, true, a surgeon this instant,” was darting away, when Anne eagerly suggested—
#*: “Captain Benwick, would not it be better for Captain Benwick? {{...}}”
#* '''1932''', {{w|William Faulkner}}, ''{{w|Light in August}}'', New York: Modern Library, 1968, Chapter 12, p. 254,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/lightinaugustmod00will]</sup>
#*: He tried to argue with her. But it was like trying to argue with a tree: she did not even '''rouse''' herself to deny, she just listened quietly and then talked again in that level, cold tone as if he had never spoken.
#* '''1980''', {{w|J. M. Coetzee}}, ''{{w|Waiting for the Barbarians}}'', Penguin, 1982, p. 108,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/waitingforbarbar00coet]</sup>
#*: The words they stopped me from uttering may have been very paltry indeed, hardly words to '''rouse''' the rabble.
# To cause to start from a [[covert]] or lurking place.
#: ''to '''rouse''' a deer or other animal of the chase''
#* '''1590''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''{{w|The Faerie Queene}}'', London: William Ponsonbie, Book 2, Canto 11, p. 350,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A12777.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Deformed creatures, in straunge difference,
#*: Some hauing heads like Harts, some like to Snakes,
#*: Some like wilde Bores late '''rouzd''' out of the brakes,
#* {{circa|1609}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Cymbeline}}'', Act III, Scene 3,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=cymbeline&Act=3&Scene=3&Scope=scene]</sup>
#*: Hark, the game is '''roused'''!
#* '''1713''', {{w|Alexander Pope}}, ''Windsor-Forest'', London: Bernard Lintott, p. 7,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004809340.0001.000]</sup>
#*: The Youth rush eager to the Sylvan War;
#*: Swarm o’er the Lawns, the Forest Walks surround,
#*: '''Rowze''' the fleet Hart, and chear the opening Hound.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To [[pull]] by main [[strength]]; to [[haul]].
#* '''1832''', {{w|Frederick Marryat}}, ''Newton Forster; or, The Merchant Service'', London: James Cochrane, Volume 1, Chapter 5, p. 71,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/newtonforsteror00marrgoog]</sup>
#*: Tom, you and the boy '''rouse''' the cable up—get about ten fathoms on deck, and bend it.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To raise; to make erect.
#* '''1590''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''{{w|The Faerie Queene}}'', London: William Ponsonbie, Book 1, Canto 11, p. 157,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A12777.0001.001]</sup>
#*: And ouer, all with brasen scales was armd,
#*: Like plated cote of steele, so couched neare,
#*: That nought mote perce, ne might his corse bee harmd
#*: With dint of swerd, nor push of pointed speare,
#*: Which as an Eagle, seeing pray appeare,
#*: His aery plumes doth '''rouze''', full rudely dight,
#*: So shaked he, that horror was to heare,
#*: For as the clashing of an Armor bright,
#*: Such noyse his '''rouzed''' scales did send vnto the knight.
#* {{circa|1598}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Henry V (play)|Henry V}}'', Act IV, Scene 3,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=henry5&Act=4&Scene=3&Scope=scene]</sup>
#*: He that outlives this day, and comes safe home,
#*: Will stand a tip-toe when the day is named,
#*: And '''rouse''' him at the name of Crispian.
# {{lb|en|slang|when followed by "on"}} To [[tell off]]; to [[criticise]].
#: ''He '''roused''' on her for being late yet again.''

