===Etymology 2===
{{defdate|Late 16th Century}} From {{m|en|carouse}}, from [[Appendix:Glossary#rebracketing|rebracketing]] of the phrase “drink carouse” as “drink a '''rouse'''”.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An official [[ceremony]] over drinks.
#* {{circa|1600}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Hamlet}}'', Act I, Scene 2,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=hamlet&Act=1&Scene=2&Scope=scene]</sup>
#*: No jocund health that Denmark drinks to-day
#*: But the great cannon to the clouds shall tell,
#*: And the King’s '''rouse''' the heaven shall bruit again,
#*: Respeaking earthly thunder.
# A [[carousal]]; a festival; a drinking frolic.
#* '''1842''', {{w|Alfred, Lord Tennyson}}, “The Vision of Sin” in ''Poems'', London: Edward Moxon, Volume 2, p. 219,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/Tennysonpoems1842vol2]</sup>
#*: Fill the cup, and fill the can:
#*: Have a '''rouse''' before the morn:
#*: Every minute dies a man,
#*: Every minute one is born.
# [[wine|Wine]] or other [[liquor]] considered an inducement to [[mirth]] or drunkenness; a full glass; a [[bumper]].

